    "answer": {
      "short": "$y=2+x\\log_2 6-\\log_2 9$, for any $x\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nRearrange for $2^y$ and take base-two logarithms. There are infinitely many real solutions; $(2,4)$ is one.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4\\times6^x&=9\\times2^y\\\\2^y&=\\frac49\\,6^x\\\\\\log_2(2^y)&=\\log_2\\left(\\frac49\\,6^x\\right)\\\\y&=2+x\\log_2 6-\\log_2 9.\\end{align*}$$\nFor every real $x$, the quantity $\\frac49\\,6^x$ is positive, so this gives a real $y$ and describes all real solutions.\n\nFor example, $(x,y)=(2,4)$ gives $4\\times36-9\\times16=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
